  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00007` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path** (`sync_io_in_hotpath`, `src/api/files.ts`, lines 12–15, confidence 0.9).\n\nSummary, verbatim:\n\n> 3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``\n> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`\n> - ``swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it``\n\n## The specific methods\n\nThree, all from `node:fs`, all inside `loadPlanSchema` (`src/api/files.ts:11`):\n\n| Line | Call | What it costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | one blocking `stat` syscall |\n| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | a second blocking `stat` on the same path |\n| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\")` | blocking `open`/`read`/`close` for the whole file |\n\nThat's **three blocking filesystem round-trips per invocation**, on the main thread. Under concurrent load every one of them parks the event loop — no other request progresses, no timers fire, no sockets are read. This is exactly the p99-spike-plus-event-loop-lag signature you're seeing: throughput is fine when requests arrive one at a time and collapses the moment they overlap, because the blocking time serialises across all in-flight requests.\n\n## The smallest async migration\n\n`loadPlanSchema` has **no in-repo callers** — it's an exported entry point, so the only consumers are external. That makes the signature constraint real, and it also means the whole change is contained in one function body.\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  let body: string;\n  try {\n    body = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n  } catch (err) {\n    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === \"ENOENT\") return null;\n    throw err;\n  }\n  if (body.length === 0) return null;\n  return JSON.parse(body);\n}\n```\n\nBehaviour mapping, branch for branch:\n\n- `existsSync` → `false` → `null` **becomes** `ENOENT` → `null`\n- `stat.size === 0` → `null` **becomes** `body.length === 0` → `null` (`size` is bytes and `length` is UTF-16 units, but for the *zero* case they're equivalent — an empty file is the empty string and nothing else is)\n- `JSON.parse` throws on malformed content — unchanged\n- Directory at that path: old code threw `EISDIR` from `readFileSync`; new code throws `EISDIR` from `readFile`. Unchanged.\n\nTwo things improve for free: the three syscall round-trips collapse to **one**, and the TOCTOU window between `existsSync`/`statSync` and `readFileSync` disappears — the old version could pass both guards and then throw `ENOENT` if the file was removed in between.\n\n### What changes for callers\n\nName and arity are identical; the parameter list is still empty. The return type necessarily widens from `unknown` to `Promise<unknown>` — there is no way to perform non-blocking I/O and still hand back a resolved value synchronously, so any honest fix requires callers to `await`. That is the entire migration cost: `loadPlanSchema()` → `await loadPlanSchema()`. Since it's an exported API with external consumers, it's a breaking change and wants a major bump.\n\n### One parity decision to make deliberately\n\n`existsSync` swallows *every* error, not just `ENOENT` — under the old code a permissions failure (`EACCES`) also returned `null`. The version above rethrows it. I'd keep that: silently returning \"no schema\" when the file exists but can't be read is a bug the old shape was hiding. If you want bit-exact parity instead, return `null` for any `err.code` rather than testing for `ENOENT` — but do it knowingly.\n\n## Explicitly not doing\n\nNo memoised promise, no module-level cache, no `SCHEMA_PATH` result stored across requests. Caching would mask the blocking call on the second request onward while leaving the first one — and every cache miss, eviction, and cold start — with the same event-loop stall. The blocking call is the defect; it's now gone.\n\n## Two notes\n\nThe same file carries `crime_00019` — `SCHEMA_PATH` is `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` `` — so on every machine except one this function currently returns `null` immediately and the blocking cost never even materialises in that environment. Whichever environment is actually stalling has a real file at a real path; worth confirming which, because the path should move behind config in the same change.\n\nWorth flagging for the scanner itself: this finding is ranked **7th** with `severity: 0.4` / `agent_risk: 0.38`, below several naming and metadata findings — despite being the one finding in the report that maps to a live production incident. The evidence is exactly right; the ranking under-weights it.",
